How procurement teams can use the research
Component qualification is a central business consideration. A part used in a spacecraft may require more extensive documentation and evaluation than a product installed in a ground terminal. Engineers and procurement specialists should define the appropriate requirements before comparing prices, since the cheapest part in a catalog may not offer the necessary traceability, reliability evidence or supply continuity.

Equipment developers also have to account for the cost of changing suppliers after a design has been qualified. Different packaging, electrical interfaces or supporting software can result in additional development work. Purchasing teams should identify which functions offer multiple viable sources and which depend on a specialized component. This is relevant when estimating lead times for a programme whose equipment will be built across several years.

For suppliers, it is useful to separate recurring equipment demand from development projects that need limited quantities for testing. The number of announced satellite systems is an imperfect proxy for component sales because projects may have different funding, qualification schedules and deployment plans. A market analysis organized by application and customer helps identify where a supplier's specific portfolio is relevant.

